python3 - <<'PY'
p='api/backend.go'
s=open(p).read()
old='''	// Partial is called with each in-progress frame, in order, before
	// Generate returns. An error from it stops the run and is returned from
	// Generate -- which is how a client hanging up mid-stream stops costing
	// the device, and is the one cancellation point an image request has.
	Partial func(ImagePartial) error'''
new='''	// Partial is called with each in-progress frame, in order, before
	// Generate returns. An error from it -- a client that hung up mid-stream
	// is the one that happens -- stops any *further* frames and is what
	// Generate returns.
	//
	// It does not stop the run. A denoising step is a submit-and-fence with
	// no cancellation point in it, so a request that is abandoned halfway
	// still costs the device the whole image; what it stops costing is the
	// encoding and the writing. That is the same bargain the non-streaming
	// path already makes, and it is written down in API.md rather than fixed.
	Partial func(ImagePartial) error'''
assert old in s
s=s.replace(old,new)
open(p,'w').write(s)
PY
go build ./... && echo OK

python3 - <<'PY'
p='zimage/vae/tiny_gpu.go'
s=open(p).read()
old = '''// **The activation arena is host-cached and the full decoder's is not.** That
// is deliberate and it is the one residency decision in here. A preview's
// whole output crosses back to the host -- 12.6 MB at 1024x1024, and eight
// times per image rather than once -- and on this machine a mapped
// write-combined buffer reads at 0.18 GB/s against a HOST_CACHED one's ~25
// (LLM.md L6b). At the write-combined rate that readback alone would be 70 ms,
// a third of what the whole decode costs; at the cached rate it is under a
// millisecond. The device reads every memory type at the same 236 GB/s, so
// the choice costs the graph nothing.'''
new = '''// **The activation arena is host-cached and the full decoder's is not, and
// the measurement says it does not matter here.** A preview's whole output
// crosses back to the host -- 12.6 MB at 1024x1024, eight times per image
// rather than once -- and LLM.md L6b measured a mapped write-combined buffer
// reading at 0.18 GB/s against a HOST_CACHED one's ~25, which would make that
// readback 70 ms against an 87 ms decode. It is not, and stage 9's note
// (research/stage-9-head-and-tail.md) explains why: the 0.18 GB/s heap is the
// *device-local* one, whose budget on this part is about 8 GB, and a program
// past it gets ordinary cached system memory whichever type it asks for. A
// pipeline holding 25 GB is well past it.
//
// ZIMAGE_TINY_UNCACHED=1 is the control, and it measures the null result:
// 87.7/88.2/87.1 ms against the cached arena's 90.9/87.7/88.3 in a 25 GB
// process, and 87.7 against 86.6 in cmd/vaebench. The cached type is kept
// anyway because it is free -- the device reads every type at 236 GB/s
// (LLM.md L0b) -- and because the arithmetic *would* bite in a process small
// enough to stay in the device-local heap, which a future benchmark or a
// smaller deployment could be.'''
